gemma4: tool-call object keys containing spaces are left unquoted by the parser and the whole call is dropped as an empty response
When a Gemma 4 tool call has an object key containing spaces, the response comes back with empty `content`, no `tool_calls`, and `finish_reason: "stop"` — on both `/api/chat` and `/v1/chat/completions`. The generated call is silently discarded; the only trace is a server-side `slog.Warn`, which for a cloud model (`gemma4:31b-cloud`) the user never sees at all.
Keys with spaces are common in real tool schemas: the trigger here was an n8n `connections` map, which is keyed by node *display names* such as `"Basic LLM Chain"` and `"Ollama Chat Model"`. An agent (Hermes) asked to copy a workflow got five "empty responses" in a row and gave up. Renaming those two nodes to `Chain` / `Model` — nothing else changed — makes the identical request succeed.
Why
`quoteGemma4BareKeys` in `model/parsers/gemma4.go` only quotes a bare key when `gemma4BareKeyEnd` stops directly on a `:`, and `gemma4BareKeyEnd` stops at the first rune that is not a letter, digit or `_`:
func gemma4BareKeyEnd(s string, start int) int {
for i < len(s) {
r, size := utf8.DecodeRuneInString(s[i:])
if !(r == '_' || unicode.IsLetter(r) || unicode.IsDigit(r)) {
So `Basic LLM Chain:` scans as the key `Basic`, sees a space rather than `:`, is left unquoted, `json.Unmarshal` fails with `invalid character 'B' looking for beginning of object key string`, `repairGemma4ToolCallArgs` has no path for it either, and `parseGemma4ToolCall` returns an error that the caller turns into zero events (`gemma4 tool call parsing failed` / `

gemma4 renderer drops tool parameters named `description`, `type`, `properties`, `required` or `nullable`
*NOTE:* This issue found and reported by LLM:
The Go renderer for gemma4 never shows the model a tool parameter whose name is `description`, `type`, `properties`, `required` or `nullable`. The model then leaves that parameter out of its tool calls, even when the parameter is required.
Cause. In `model/renderers/gemma4.go`, `writeSchemaProperties` skips property names with `isSchemaStandardKey`:
for _, name := range keys {
if isSchemaStandardKey(name) {
continue
}
func isSchemaStandardKey(key string) bool {
switch key {
case "description", "type", "properties", "required", "nullable":
return true
`writeSchemaProperties` is called for a tool's top-level parameters (through `writeTypedProperties`), for nested object properties and for array items. The skip is therefore applied to every `properties` map. In those maps the keys are parameter names, not schema keywords.
This differs from the upstream template the renderer is checked against. In `model/renderers/testdata/gemma4_31b_chat_template.jinja2`, the macro is `format_parameters(properties, required, filter_keys=false)`. The top-level call is `format_parameters(params['properties'], params['required'])`, which keeps the default `filter_keys=false`. Only one call site passes `filter_keys=true`: `format_parameters(value, value['required'] | default([]), filter_keys=true)`. None of the reference tests in `gemma4_reference_test.go` use a parameter with one of these names, so the difference is not caught.
